  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 116 AM EST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nCold northwest flow has enveloped the area and has brewed up a batch \nof lake effect cloud streets. Seasonably crisp early this morning \nwith temperatures currently in the 40s, headed toward the 30s as \ncold air advection persists. Upstream, a shortwave is noted over the \narrowhead of MN. This feature drops south through the day and by \nsunset this secondary push of cold air brings scattered rain and \nsnow showers mainly along and north the Toll Road; lower confidence \nsouth of there. Rain will be primary precipitation type early in the \nevening, but as the hours tick on beyond sunset, snow will be more \nlikely to mix in. This shortwaves moves quickly such that residence \ntime of precipitation will generally be brief; moisture profiles are \nshallow as well. Dry conditions resume soon after midnight as high \npressure undercuts precipitation. \n\nDry for Tuesday and Wednesday with seasonably cold conditions as high \npressure moves over the Great Lakes. Meanwhile, to the west, a deep \ntrough encases the central US with a pair of 500-mb lows noted. \nThese lows move over Midwest Wednesday night through Thursday. Sharp \nwarm air advection on the eastern flank of the interior-most low \nfavors primarily rain for our area Thursday. The magnitude of any \ncold air is currently locked up elsewhere. However, as the low \nmatures over the Northeast US, cold air advection behind this low \nimproves somewhat to permit some snow Thursday night into Friday. \nThis is highly uncertain given the modest magnitude of cold air and \nour sensitive location on the western edge of this low. This has \ncertainly been a quick ramp-up in POPs, now topping 60-80% \n(primarily rain) Wednesday night through at least Friday morning. \nDon't let social media fool you; this does not appear to be a \nblockbuster storm locally by any stretch. \n\nTroughing continues for the remainder of the holiday weekend keeping \ntemperatures seasonably cool and we could see one or more shortwaves \nbreeze by. Our seasonal whiplash returns next week as a trough out \nwest buckles the downstream jet, sending our high temperatures back \ntoward 50 degrees by the debut of December.",
